Qore dependency backports for Ubuntu 26.04
========================================

Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

The testing PPA is:

  https://launchpad.net/~qore-packagers/+archive/ubuntu/qore-testing
  dput destination: ppa:qore-packagers/qore-testing

These recipes initially target Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute). They are not Debian,
Ubuntu 24.04, or Ubuntu 22.04 qualification results. Build each distribution
separately and resolve its dependencies before adding another target here.

Sources and package scope
-------------------------

sources.json pins upstream archives and existing Debian/Ubuntu packaging by
HTTPS URL and SHA-256 digest. It records the changelog date, target distribution,
maintainer, and PPA versions. The preparation tool additionally verifies the
c-ares, nghttp2, nghttp3, and ngtcp2 upstream signatures using keys shipped in
the pinned Debian packaging. The tree-sitter GitHub tag archive is pinned by
SHA-256; it does not have a release archive signature in this recipe.

  c-ares 1.34.8: Debian library packaging, plus both Qore DNS query fixes.
  nghttp2 1.70.0: Debian library, application, and documentation packages.
  nghttp3 1.18.0: Debian library packaging updated from 1.17.0.
  ngtcp2 1.25.0: Debian packaging updated from 1.24.0, including both OpenSSL
    and GnuTLS crypto backends.
  qore-tree-sitter 0.26.13: C runtime and development packages only, retaining
    Debian's SONAME-qualified library package and symbol history.

qore-tree-sitter is deliberately a separate source package. It produces
libtree-sitter0.26 and libtree-sitter-dev, without backporting Rust crates or the
tree-sitter CLI. Qore builds its committed generated parser directly. It does
not need the generator installed to build astparser. The C runtime has an
offline C API/linking smoke test and an installed-package test; this does not
replace upstream's full Rust/parser regression suite.

c-ares patch maintenance
------------------------

The source preparer generates debian/patches/qore-lost-query.patch using
cmake/PatchCaresLostQuery.cmake from this Qore checkout. This is the same
patcher used by the ordinary FetchContent build. Both changes are required:

  * A second detach must not remove a different query which reused its ID.
  * The send path must compare the query identity, rather than just checking
    whether that query ID is present, to avoid accessing a freed query.

The generated patch is added to Debian's quilt series and included in the
source upload. It is applied by dpkg-source, without CMake downloading or
patching libraries during the Qore package build. A changed upstream source
that no longer matches the patcher fails preparation and requires review.

When updating c-ares, check whether upstream includes BOTH fixes before
removing the patch. Also increment the PPA revision when modifying a published
patch. Keep the system-library backport and FetchContent fixes synchronized.

Preparing unsigned source uploads
--------------------------------

Use the distribution's /usr/bin/python3 (3.11 or newer, with tarfile's data
extraction filter), dpkg-dev, cmake, gpg and gpgv. On Ubuntu, install these with
APT. Generated archives and extracted sources must live outside the checkout:

  tools/prepare-debian-backports.py \
    --output /path/to/new-source-workspace \
    --cache /path/to/download-cache

Pass source package names after the options to prepare a subset. The tool
refuses existing package workspaces. After populating the download cache,
--offline repeats preparation without network access in another new workspace.

Only the pristine SOURCE preparation uses dpkg-buildpackage -d -nc; native
Build-Depends need not be installed to assemble an upload. Binary builds must
resolve Build-Depends and run their tests. The source uploads include the orig
tarballs (-sa), upstream signatures where available, .dsc, .debian.tar.xz,
source .buildinfo and source .changes. They are unsigned.

The source versions sort above the required upstream version and below the
corresponding future official Debian revision. Do not reuse a version after
Launchpad has accepted it; increment its Qore revision in sources.json.

Clean local binary builds
-------------------------

The helper uses rootless Podman with an Ubuntu 26.04 image. It installs tools
and declared build dependencies in a fresh container, then builds and tests
as an unprivileged build user from the .dsc with the network disabled. It does
not install backports into the development host or change the host's APT sources.

  podman pull docker.io/library/ubuntu:26.04
  tools/build-debian-backport.sh \
    /path/to/new-source-workspace/c-ares /path/to/new-c-ares-results

Repeat for nghttp2, nghttp3, ngtcp2 and qore-tree-sitter. Each results directory
must be new. Results include binaries, build metadata, logs, the base image's
identity and the exact build-dependency inventory. The provisioned build image
is retained locally for diagnosis; its ID is recorded in build-image.txt.
QORE_BACKPORT_IMAGE may select a previously pulled Ubuntu 26.04 base image by
digest. No image is pushed to a registry.

Lintian errors in source or binary packages fail the build helper. Review
warnings as well: inherited nghttp2 packaging includes a metapackage with no
payload and Sphinx JavaScript
copies in its documentation. Ubuntu 26.04's Lintian also reports the newer
Standards-Version and backport revision conventions in some source packages.
Passing this helper is not a claim of full
autopkgtest, installation/upgrade, multi-architecture, or reproducibility
qualification. The equivalent official builder is sbuild:

  sbuild --chroot-mode=schroot -d resolute --arch=amd64 -A /path/to/package.dsc

Before uploading, verify the resulting source package with:

  lintian --fail-on error /path/to/package_source.changes

Signing and uploading
---------------------

Source preparation and local builds do not contact Launchpad to publish
anything. Review the source changes, build results and PPA target first.
Transfer each source upload's complete set of files to the host holding the
registered signing key. Keep the private key on that host. For each reviewed
source .changes file, on that host:

  debsign -k FULL_PRIMARY_FINGERPRINT /path/to/package_source.changes
  dput ppa:qore-packagers/qore-testing /path/to/package_source.changes

Use the SOURCE .changes files from the preparation workspace, not the binary
.changes files from the build results. Each uploader signs with their own
registered key and must have upload rights to the team's PPA. Check the PPA's
enabled architectures against the architectures actually qualified locally.
Local validation initially uses native amd64.

Wait for all dependency binaries to build and publish for resolute before
uploading Qore. Qore's Build-Depends can then be resolved from the same PPA.
The separately packaged YAML module is still needed for qore-debug-tools.

